Below are the changes for the current release.
See the CHANGES file for changes in older releases.
See the RELEASENOTES file for a summary of changes in each release.
Issue # numbers mentioned below can be found on Github. For more details, add
the issue number to the end of the URL: https://github.com/swig/swig/issues/

Version 4.6.0 (in progress)
===========================

2026-10-16: vadz
            [Python] Fix "metaclass conflict" TypeError when importing a module with a class using
            the nondynamic feature that derives from a nondynamic class from another module.

2026-10-16: vadz
            [Python] Fix accessing the elements of a wrapped STL container of a class using the
            nondynamic feature, which raised AttributeError.

2026-10-16: vadz
            [Python] Setting a property of a class using the nondynamic feature no longer calls its
            getter, so it works even when the getter throws, e.g. because the value is not set yet.

2026-10-14: wsfulton
            [C, C#, D, Go, Guile, Java, Lua, PHP] An exception caught by the catch all handler that
            %catches(...) adds is now raised as a SWIG_RuntimeError with the message "unknown exception",
            as in the other target languages. Before, it was rethrown with warning 468 unless the interface
            file included exception.i. To rethrow it as before, add this to the interface file:

              %typemap(throws) (...) "throw;"

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-14: wsfulton
            %include <exception.i> is no longer needed and the file now does nothing. The 'throws' typemap
            for '...' used by %catches(...) is now always available. Every C++ wrapper now includes the
            <stdexcept> and <typeinfo> headers needed by SWIG_CATCH_STDEXCEPT and SWIG_CATCH_UNKNOWN.

2026-10-14: wsfulton
            [Guile] A global variable that is an array of unknown size, such as 'extern int b[];' or
            'extern char name[];', now gives warning 462, as in the other target languages. Setting it
            still raises wrong-type-arg.

2026-10-14: wsfulton
            [Python] Fix a memory leak with -castmode of a std::pair made from a tuple whose first element
            is converted with a cast of higher rank than its second, such as a float for an int.

2026-10-13: wsfulton
            [R] NULL now selects an overloaded function taking a char array, such as 'char [4]' or
            'char []', as it does for any other array.

2026-10-13: wsfulton
            [Octave, Perl, Python, Ruby, Scilab, Tcl] A string now selects an overloaded function taking a
            reference to a char array, such as 'char (&)[4]' or 'char (&&)[4]', as it does for 'char [4]'.

2026-10-13: wsfulton
            [Guile, Lua, PHP] A string now selects an overloaded function taking a char array, such as
            'char [4]', as it does for 'char *'. In Guile it now does for 'char []' too. In PHP, null now
            selects one too, as for any other array.

2026-10-13: wsfulton
            [OCaml] Setting a read only member or global variable now raises Failure "Unable to set 'x', as
            it is read only" instead of being silently ignored.

2026-10-13: wsfulton
            [R] Setting a read only member variable, or a name that is not a member variable, with $<- or
            [[<- now gives an error instead of a stack overflow ("C stack usage" or "node stack overflow").
            Getting a name that is not a member variable or method with $ now gives NULL, also instead of a
            stack overflow.

2026-10-13: wsfulton
            [PHP] Setting a read only member variable now throws an Error "Cannot modify readonly property
            Foo::$x", as for a PHP readonly property, instead of "Invalid callback , no array or string
            given".

2026-10-13: wsfulton
            [C] Fix a derived class having a set function for an inherited read only member variable. The
            wrapper code did not compile for a const member variable and a member variable made read only
            with %immutable could be set.

2026-10-13: wsfulton
            [Scilab] Remove the extra ';' after the gateway function in the generated code for Scilab 6 and
            later, which gives an "extra ';' outside of a function" warning with -Wextra-semi in C++.

2026-10-13: wsfulton
            [Perl] Fix reading a global variable of pointer, array, reference or class type changing a
            pointer object assigned to it. After a failed set, such as setting an 'int *' variable to a
            'double *' pointer object, reading the variable changed that object to hold the variable's
            pointer and gave it as the variable's value. After a successful set, a later change to the
            variable in C changed the assigned pointer object too. The variable no longer holds a
            reference to the assigned pointer object, so, as in the other target languages, the object
            must be kept alive for as long as C uses the pointer.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-13: wsfulton
            [Lua] nil for an INPUT or INOUT array parameter using the typemaps.i array typemaps, such as
            'int INPUT[ANY]' or '(double *INOUT, int)', is now passed on to the function as a NULL
            pointer, as for any other array parameter, instead of raising an error. The length of a
            variable size array is then 0 and an INOUT array gives back nil. To raise an error for nil as
            before, add a check typemap for each array parameter, for example:

              %typemap(check) double *arr %{
                if (!$1) {
                  SWIG_Lua_pusherrstring(L, "expected a table");
                  SWIG_fail;
                }
              %}

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-13: wsfulton
            [Javascript] null for an array parameter using the arrays_javascript.i typemaps, such as
            'int []' or 'double [4]', is now passed on to the function as a NULL pointer, as for any other
            array parameter, instead of raising an error. Setting an array variable to null still raises
            an error. To raise an error for a null array parameter as before, add a check typemap for each
            array type, for example:

              %typemap(check) int[], int[ANY] %{
                if (!$1) {
                  SWIG_exception(SWIG_ERROR, "null array");
                }
              %}

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-13: wsfulton
            [Java] A null Java array for an array parameter using the arrays_java.i typemaps, such as
            'int []' or an array of classes from JAVA_ARRAYSOFCLASSES, is now passed on to the function as
            a NULL pointer, as for any other array parameter, instead of throwing a NullPointerException.
            Setting an array variable to null still throws a NullPointerException. To throw one for a null
            array parameter as before, add a check typemap for each array type, for example:

              %typemap(check) int[], int[ANY] %{
                if (!$1) {
                  SWIG_JavaThrowException(jenv, SWIG_JavaNullPointerException, "null array");
                  return $null;
                }
              %}

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-13: wsfulton
            An rvalue reference to an array, such as 'int (&&)[4]', is now wrapped as a reference to an
            array, as a parameter or as a member or global variable. The wrapper code did not compile,
            except for an array of char in some target languages. As for a reference to an array, NULL
            gives a NULL reference error, an array of char is a string and no ownership is taken.

2026-10-13: wsfulton
            [C] NULL for a reference to a primitive type, bool or an enum, such as 'int &' or 'int *&', now
            gives a NULL reference error instead of passing a NULL reference to the wrapped function.

2026-10-13: wsfulton
            [Perl] Fix a crash reading a global variable of pointer, array, reference or class type after
            setting it to undef or after a failed set, such as setting a read only array variable.

2026-10-13: wsfulton
            A global or member variable that is a reference to an array of unknown size, such as
            'int (&)[]', is now read only with warning 462, as is an array of unknown size, since there is
            no size to bound a copy of the elements. The wrapper code for setting it did not compile.

2026-10-13: wsfulton
            [C, Go, OCaml] NULL for a reference parameter, such as 'Foo &' or 'int (&)[4]', now gives a
            NULL reference error, as in the other target languages, instead of passing a NULL reference to
            the wrapped function.

2026-10-11: wsfulton
            [Javascript, Octave, Perl, Python, R, Ruby, Scilab, Tcl] SWIG_CATCH_STDEXCEPT now catches
            std::bad_cast and raises SWIG_TypeError, as it already did in the other target languages,
            instead of SWIG_SystemError from catching std::exception. This is for better consistency of
            the error handling common to all target languages, which now has a single definition of
            SWIG_CATCH_STDEXCEPT. To raise SWIG_SystemError as before, redefine SWIG_CATCH_STDEXCEPT in the
            interface file:

              #undef SWIG_CATCH_STDEXCEPT
              %define SWIG_CATCH_STDEXCEPT
                catch (std::invalid_argument& e) {
                  SWIG_exception(SWIG_ValueError, e.what());
                } catch (std::domain_error& e) {
                  SWIG_exception(SWIG_ValueError, e.what());
                } catch (std::overflow_error& e) {
                  SWIG_exception(SWIG_OverflowError, e.what());
                } catch (std::out_of_range& e) {
                  SWIG_exception(SWIG_IndexError, e.what());
                } catch (std::length_error& e) {
                  SWIG_exception(SWIG_IndexError, e.what());
                } catch (std::runtime_error& e) {
                  SWIG_exception(SWIG_RuntimeError, e.what());
                } catch (std::exception& e) {
                  SWIG_exception(SWIG_SystemError, e.what());
                }
              %enddef

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-11: wsfulton
            SWIG_exception can now be used anywhere without %include <exception.i>, such as in a typemap or
            in %exception code, as its support code is now always included, for example:

              %typemap(check) int positive {
                if ($1 <= 0)
                  SWIG_exception(SWIG_ValueError, "Expected a positive value.");
              }

            SWIG_exception_fail is now available in every target language, as the same macro as
            SWIG_exception. This is part of a refactor for consistent error handling, including in the
            typemaps common to all target languages.

2026-10-11: wsfulton
            [R] SWIG_exception now raises an R error for SWIG_IndexError, as for the other error codes,
            instead of returning NA. This is part of a refactor for consistent error handling, including
            in the typemaps common to all target languages. To return NA as before, redefine
            SWIG_exception in the interface file. Also redefine SWIG_exception_fail, which the SWIG
            library typemaps use, to keep its behaviour:

              %{
              #undef SWIG_exception
              #define SWIG_exception(code, msg) do { if ((code) == SWIG_IndexError) \
                return Rf_ScalarLogical(NA_LOGICAL); SWIG_Error(code, msg); SWIG_fail; } while (0)
              #undef SWIG_exception_fail
              #define SWIG_exception_fail(code, msg) do { SWIG_Error(code, msg); SWIG_fail; } while (0)
              %}

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-11: wsfulton
            [Ruby] SWIG_exception now jumps to the wrapper's fail label after raising the error, as
            SWIG_exception_fail does, so it can only be used in a wrapper function, as in the other target
            languages. As rb_raise does not return, it could be used elsewhere before, such as in the body
            of an %extend method or in a %{ %} function. This is part of a refactor for consistent error
            handling, including in the typemaps common to all target languages. To use it elsewhere as
            before, redefine SWIG_exception in the interface file. Also redefine SWIG_exception_fail, which
            the SWIG library typemaps use, to keep its behaviour:

              %{
              #undef SWIG_exception
              #define SWIG_exception(code, msg) do { SWIG_Error(code, msg); } while (0)
              #undef SWIG_exception_fail
              #define SWIG_exception_fail(code, msg) do { SWIG_Error(code, msg); SWIG_fail; } while (0)
              %}

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-11: wsfulton
            [Javascript] With Node-API and NAPI_CPP_EXCEPTIONS, SWIG_exception now jumps to the wrapper's
            fail label after throwing the error, as SWIG_exception_fail does, so it can only be used in a
            wrapper function, as in the other target languages. As the error is a C++ exception, it could be
            used elsewhere before, such as in the body of an %extend method or in a %{ %} function. This is
            part of a refactor for consistent error handling, including in the typemaps common to all target
            languages. To use it elsewhere as before, redefine SWIG_exception in the interface file. Also
            redefine SWIG_exception_fail, which the SWIG library typemaps use, to keep its behaviour:

              %{
              #undef SWIG_exception
              #define SWIG_exception(code, msg) SWIG_Error(code, msg)
              #undef SWIG_exception_fail
              #define SWIG_exception_fail(code, msg) do { SWIG_Error(code, msg); SWIG_fail; } while (0)
              %}

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-11: wsfulton
            [Tcl] SWIG_exception now leaves the wrapper through its cleanup code, as SWIG_fail does, so any
            memory allocated for the arguments is freed. It returned TCL_ERROR directly before. This
            corrects the error handling as part of a refactor for consistent error handling, including in
            the typemaps common to all target languages. To return directly as before, redefine
            SWIG_exception in the interface file. Also redefine SWIG_exception_fail, which the SWIG library
            typemaps use, to keep its behaviour:

              %{
              #undef SWIG_exception
              #define SWIG_exception(code, msg) do { SWIG_Error(code, msg); return TCL_ERROR; } while (0)
              #undef SWIG_exception_fail
              #define SWIG_exception_fail(code, msg) do { SWIG_Error(code, msg); SWIG_fail; } while (0)
              %}

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-11: wsfulton
            [Scilab] SWIG_exception now leaves the wrapper after setting the error. It carried on with the
            rest of the wrapper before. This corrects the error handling as part of a refactor for
            consistent error handling, including in the typemaps common to all target languages. To carry
            on after the error as before, redefine SWIG_exception in the interface file. Also redefine
            SWIG_exception_fail, which the SWIG library typemaps use, to keep its behaviour:

              %{
              #undef SWIG_exception
              #define SWIG_exception(code, msg) SWIG_Scilab_Error(code, msg)
              #undef SWIG_exception_fail
              #define SWIG_exception_fail(code, msg) do { SWIG_Error(code, msg); SWIG_fail; } while (0)
              %}

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-11: wsfulton
            [C] SWIG_exception now returns from the wrapper after raising the error. It carried on with the
            rest of the wrapper before, after raising the SWIG_CException when wrapping C++ code, or after
            calling any SWIG_exception defined in C by the user when wrapping C code or using -noexcept. This
            corrects the error handling as part of a refactor for consistent error handling, including in the
            typemaps common to all target languages. To carry on as before, redefine SWIG_exception in the
            interface file before using it:

              #undef SWIG_exception
              #define SWIG_exception(code, msg) SWIG_CException_Raise(code, msg)

            When wrapping C code or using -noexcept, only the #undef is needed.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-11: wsfulton
            [C#] SWIG_exception now throws an ArgumentNullException for SWIG_NullReferenceError, as the null
            checks in the C# typemaps do, instead of a NullReferenceException. This is part of a refactor for
            consistent error handling, including in the typemaps common to all target languages. To throw a
            NullReferenceException as before, redefine SWIG_exception in the interface file before using it:

              #undef SWIG_exception
              #define SWIG_exception(code, msg) { if ((code) == SWIG_NullReferenceError) \
                SWIG_CSharpSetPendingException(SWIG_CSharpNullReferenceException, msg); \
                else SWIG_CSharpException(code, msg); return $null; }

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-11: wsfulton
            [Guile] SWIG_exception now raises an error with a symbol as its key, such as swig-type-error,
            so it can be caught by name. It used a string as the key before, which Guile does not accept,
            so a wrong-type-arg error was raised instead. SWIG_NullReferenceError now gives misc-error, as
            for the null checks in the Guile typemaps. This is part of a refactor for consistent error
            handling, including in the typemaps common to all target languages. To raise the same errors
            as before, redefine SWIG_exception in the interface file:

              %{
              SWIGINTERN void SWIG_exception_string_key(int code, const char *msg, const char *subr) {
                static const char *keys[] = { "swig-error", "swig-io-error", "swig-runtime-error",
                  "swig-index-error", "swig-type-error", "swig-division-by-zero", "swig-overflow-error",
                  "swig-syntax-error", "swig-value-error", "swig-system-error", "swig-error",
                  "swig-memory-error", "swig-null-reference-error" };
                const char *key = code <= -1 && code >= -13 ? keys[-code - 1] : "swig-error";
                scm_error(scm_from_locale_string(key), subr, msg, SCM_EOL, SCM_BOOL_F);
              }
              #undef SWIG_exception
              #define SWIG_exception(code, msg) SWIG_exception_string_key(code, msg, FUNC_NAME)
              %}

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-10: wsfulton
            [OCaml] An array, such as 'int [4]', 'Foo [2]' or 'int [2][3]', is now wrapped as a
            pointer to its first element by default, as in the other target languages. An array
            parameter, member variable or global variable failed with an error such as "Array
            arguments for arbitrary types need a typemap", unless array typemaps, such as those in
            carray.i, were used. C_void is passed as a NULL pointer for an array parameter. Setting
            an array member or global variable copies the elements, raising Invalid_argument for
            NULL. A global variable that is an array of unknown size, such as 'int []', is read only
            and gives warning 462.

2026-10-10: wsfulton
            [PHP] NULL is now passed as a NULL pointer for a 'char INPUT[ANY]' parameter with the
            typemaps.i typemaps, as for a 'char *' parameter, rather than as an empty string.

2026-10-10: wsfulton
            [C#] The arrays_csharp.i FIXED[] typemaps for 'long' and 'unsigned long' now use a C#
            int[] or uint[], as the INPUT[], OUTPUT[] and INOUT[] typemaps do, unless SWIGWORDSIZE64
            is defined. They always used long[] or ulong[], which passed the wrong data where long
            is 32 bits, such as on Windows.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-10: wsfulton
            [C#] Fix the arrays_csharp.i 'long' and 'unsigned long' INPUT[], OUTPUT[] and INOUT[]
            typemaps when typemaps.i is included after arrays_csharp.i. typemaps.i replaced them with
            typemaps that crashed for null and passed only the first element, so these arrays now
            behave as for the other primitive types.

2026-10-10: wsfulton
            [R] NULL now selects an overloaded function or method taking a pointer to or an array
            of numbers, bools or enums, such as 'int *', 'double []' or 'bool [3]', or an array of
            strings, such as 'char *[3]', where it gave "cannot find overloaded function". A
            character vector of enum names, such as c("One", "Two"), now also selects an overload
            taking a fixed size enum array, such as 'enum Finger [2]'.

2026-10-09: wsfulton
            [OCaml] An enum passed or returned by const reference, such as 'const Color &', is now
            converted to and from an OCaml enum value, as when passed by value, rather than being
            handled as a pointer. So is a global variable of such a type. An enum value could not be
            passed for such a parameter. A director method returning one crashed.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-09: wsfulton
            [OCaml] Fix director methods with an enum or a member pointer parameter, which gave
            wrapper code that did not compile.

2026-10-09: wsfulton
            [OCaml] Fix a crash when C_void is passed for an array with the carray.i typemaps,
            such as 'int [4]'. A NULL pointer is now passed for a parameter. Setting a member
            variable now raises Invalid_argument.

2026-10-09: wsfulton
            [Scilab] An empty matrix for a parameter that is an array of numbers, bools or strings,
            such as 'int [4]', 'double []', 'bool [4]' or 'char *[]', is now passed as a NULL
            pointer, as NULL is in the other target languages. It was passed as memory for no
            elements, which the function read past the end of, or rejected for bools and strings.
            Setting an array member or global variable, such as 'int [4]', to an empty matrix now
            gives a SWIG NullReferenceError rather than copying from it. The matrix.i typemaps for
            'char **' and a std::vector<bool> parameter also now accept an empty matrix.

2026-10-09: wsfulton
            [C#, D, Go, Guile, Java, Lua, PHP] Fix a crash when setting an array member or global
            variable, such as 'int [4]', 'Foo [2]' or 'int [2][3]', to NULL, such as null in Java,
            as the array was copied from it. An error is now raised instead, as in the other target
            languages. NULL for an array parameter is still passed as a NULL pointer.

2026-10-09: wsfulton
            [C] When wrapping C++, fix a crash when setting an array member or global variable,
            such as 'int [4]' or 'int [2][3]', to NULL. SWIG_exception is now called with
            SWIG_NullReferenceError instead, which raises a SWIG_CException.

2026-10-09: wsfulton
            [C#, D] The canthrow attribute now works in memberin and globalin typemaps, so that a
            pending exception set in one of these typemaps is thrown when setting the variable.

2026-10-09: wsfulton
            Fix a string literal with characters after an embedded NUL, such as "a\0bc" or
            L"a\0bc", where SWIG copies the literal into the generated code, such as for a
            constant from '#define S "a\0bc"' or %constant, or for a default argument used by
            the wrapper with compactdefaultargs. The characters after the NUL were lost, usually
            becoming NULs, as in "a\0\0\0".

2026-10-09: wsfulton
            [C#, Java] #3559 Fix a constant from a string literal holding a character that is not
            ASCII, with %csconst(1) or %javaconst(1), which was given a char for each byte of its
            UTF-8 encoding. It now holds the characters that the literal encodes.

2026-10-09: wsfulton
            [C#] Fix a static member constant initialised by a string or wide character literal
            with %csconst(1), which was given the C++ escape sequences of the literal, so did not
            compile for a character that is not printable ASCII or for a wide literal, such as:

              struct S {
                static constexpr const char *ESC = "\033";
              };

2026-10-09: wsfulton
            [C#, D, Java] A constant from a wide string or wide character literal, such as
            '#define WS L"abc"', with %csconst(1), %dmanifestconst or %javaconst(1), is now read
            from C/C++, as without the feature, when it is wrapped as a pointer, as it is without
            typemaps for wchar_t. The literal it was initialised with did not compile.

2026-10-09: wsfulton
            [D, Java] Fix a constant from a wide string or wide character literal with
            %dmanifestconst or %javaconst(1) and typemaps giving it a string or character type. It
            was initialised with the C++ wide literal, such as L"abc", so did not compile.

2026-10-09: wsfulton
            [R] An object can now be passed for an array of its class, such as a Shape for a
            'Shape [3]' parameter, as a pointer to the first element, as in the other target
            languages. Any argument other than NULL for an array of a class gave an R internal
            error such as "R_ExternalPtrAddr: argument of type S4SXP is not an external pointer".

2026-10-09: wsfulton
            [R] A vector of more than one value, such as c(6, 7), now gives a SWIG TypeError for a
            number, bool or enum parameter passed by value or by const reference, such as 'short'
            or 'const double &', which takes one value. The first value was used. An integer type or
            an enum passed by value gave the warning "using only the first element". The other types
            gave no warning.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-09: wsfulton
            [R] A character vector of more than one string, such as c("ab", "cd"), now gives a SWIG
            TypeError for a 'char *', char array, 'char' or std::string parameter, which takes one
            string, rather than the first string being used and the others ignored. An array of
            strings, such as 'char *[3]', still takes a vector of up to that many strings.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-08: wsfulton
            [R] A bool returned by reference, such as 'const bool &', or by an OUTPUT or INOUT
            typemap is now an R logical rather than an integer.

2026-10-08: wsfulton
            [R] Fix the OUTPUT and INOUT typemaps for a function returning void, which gave the R
            error "attempt to set index 0/0 in SET_VECTOR_ELT". Also fix the INOUT typemaps for a
            pointer, such as 'int *INOUT', which crashed. Fix a function returning a class pointer
            and with OUTPUT or INOUT parameters, which failed to make an object of the returned
            list. A list is returned holding the return value, if not void, followed by the output
            values.

2026-10-08: wsfulton
            [R] A wrapped object of the wrong type passed for a pointer or reference parameter now
            gives a SWIG TypeError, as in the other target languages. Any pointer was accepted and
            used as the type of the parameter, so for example passing a 'Foo' for a 'Foo *&'
            parameter crashed. An object of a class that only another module knows, such as a class
            derived in a module that %imports this one, is still accepted, as R modules do not share
            their type information.

2026-10-08: wsfulton
            [R] NULL and an empty vector, such as integer(0) or character(0), are now told apart,
            as the R wrappers no longer convert NULL to an empty vector before passing it to C/C++.
            NULL for a 'const std::vector<T> &' parameter now gives SWIG:NullReferenceError rather
            than an empty std::vector. NULL for a 'std::vector<T>' passed by value gives a SWIG
            TypeError. An empty vector is an empty std::vector, an array of zeros for a fixed size
            array and a pointer to no elements for a pointer to numbers. It gives a SWIG TypeError
            for a 'char *' string, a char array or a reference. A user scoercein typemap that
            converts its argument should leave NULL unchanged, as described in the new "NULL and
            empty vectors" section of the R chapter.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-08: wsfulton
            [R] Fix reading a fixed size array member or global variable of a numeric or enum type,
            such as 'int values[4]'. Reading an integer or enum array failed with an R error such
            as '"_p_int" is not a defined class'. Reading a float or double array gave an R external
            pointer, which could not be used in R. The array is now read as an R vector of all its
            elements, of integers, numbers or enum names, which is also what is used to set it. For
            example, for a struct member 'int values[4]':

              s$values <- c(1L, 2L, 3L, 4L)
              s$values
              [1] 1 2 3 4

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-08: wsfulton
            [R] Fix a fixed size array parameter, member or global variable of a numeric type or an
            enum, such as 'int [4]', given a vector with fewer elements than the array, which read
            past the end of the vector. The remaining elements are now 0. A vector with more
            elements than the array now gives a SWIG OverflowError.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-08: wsfulton
